  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming are under a dry west to\nnorthwest flow this evening as high pressure rebuilds along the\nPacific coast. High temperatures stayed up to 5F below seasonal\nnormals over northern portions of the area, while temperatures\nstayed abnormally hot over southern Utah. A high of 99F at Cedar\nCity tied the record high for the date, with some possibility that\nother daily records were broken. \n\nThe ridge will continue to build over the next few days, slowly \nshifting east as it does so. By Friday, the ridge is expected to \nbe overhead, and highs up to 10F above seasonal normals will be \ncommon for both Friday and Saturday, bringing the potential of an \nincrease in heat related illness. \n\nThe position of the ridge is expected to allow a subtle intrusion\nof monsoon moisture into southern Utah, potentially as early as\nThursday. This moisture will allow for the development of a few\nhigh based showers and thunderstorms, primarily focused over the\nhigher terrain of southern Utah. Any storms that develop will be\ncapable of producing gusty and erratic downburst winds. \n\nBy Sunday into Monday, there is good agreement in guidance that\nthe ridge axis will shift east of the area, becoming centered over\nthe Four Corners. This positioning of the ridge is a typical\nscenario for a larger surge of monsoon moisture across the\nforecast area. Thus, there will be decreasing temperatures and\nincrease in the potential for precipitation, with the primary\nthreat over the area, particularly southern Utah, shifting toward\nflash flooding for at least the first part of the upcoming week.",
